<section class="sec" id="sec-break">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">07</span><h2>When the Rule Breaks</h2></div>

  <p>The derivation quietly assumed the whole train stays on the span while you slide it. On a short span that is impossible, and the rule has to be abandoned rather than trusted.</p>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Counter-case</span><h4>Two 50 kN axles, 6 m apart, on an 8 m span</h4></div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div>\( R = 100 \) kN, \( \bar&#123;x&#125; = 3 \) m from axle 1 (midway, by symmetry).</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Candidate axle 1:</strong> \( e = +3 \), \( x = (8-3)/2 = 2.5 \) m. Axle 2 would then be at 8.5 m &mdash; <em>0.5 m past support B</em>. Invalid.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Candidate axle 2:</strong> \( e = -3 \), \( x = (8+3)/2 = 5.5 \) m. Axle 1 would be at &minus;0.5 m &mdash; <em>before support A</em>. Invalid.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div>Both candidates are void, so fall back on physics: with the axles 6 m apart on an 8 m span, the best you can do is put <strong>one</strong> axle at midspan and let the other sit near a support where its lever arm is small.
        <div class="eq">\[ M = \frac&#123;PL&#125;&#123;4&#125; = \frac&#123;50 \times 8&#125;&#123;4&#125; = 100\ \text&#123;kN·m&#125; \]</div></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> 100 kN&middot;m. Had you applied the formula blindly you would have written 78.1 kN&middot;m for either candidate &mdash; <strong>22% unconservative</strong>, and wrong in the direction that breaks bridges. Always run the legality check in Step 3 of the procedure.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Core Idea</span>
    <p>Everything in this chapter is one sweep in two directions. Move the load to find the worst case at a section; move the section to find the worst case in the beam. The envelope records both sweeps at once, and the bisection rule is simply the shortcut that finds its peak without doing the sweep by hand:</p>
    <div class="eq">\[ M_&#123;\text&#123;abs&#125;&#125; = \max_&#123;\text&#123;valid axles &#125;k&#125;\left[\frac&#123;R&#125;&#123;L&#125;\left(\frac&#123;L-e_k&#125;&#123;2&#125;\right)^&#123;2&#125; - M_&#123;\text&#123;left&#125;,k&#125;\right] \]</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 8 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-examples">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">08</span><h2>Worked Examples</h2></div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 1</span><h4>Two-Axle Truck on a 15 m Span</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> Axle loads of 60 kN and 40 kN, 4 m apart, cross a 15 m simply supported beam. Find the absolute maximum moment and the absolute maximum shear.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Resultant.</strong> \( R = 60+40 = 100 \) kN. Measuring from the 60 kN axle: \( \bar&#123;x&#125; = \dfrac&#123;40 \times 4&#125;&#123;100&#125; = 1.60 \) m.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Candidate: the 60 kN axle.</strong> \( e = 1.60 - 0 = +1.60 \) m, so \( x = \dfrac&#123;15-1.6&#125;&#123;2&#125; = 6.70 \) m and \( M_&#123;\text&#123;left&#125;&#125; = 0 \).
        <div class="eq">\[ M = \frac&#123;100 \times 6.70^2&#125;&#123;15&#125; = \frac&#123;4489&#125;&#123;15&#125; = 299.3\ \text&#123;kN·m&#125; \]</div>
        Legality: the 40 kN axle lands at 10.70 m &lt; 15 m. Valid.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Candidate: the 40 kN axle.</strong> \( e = 1.60 - 4 = -2.40 \) m, so \( x = \dfrac&#123;15+2.4&#125;&#123;2&#125; = 8.70 \) m and \( M_&#123;\text&#123;left&#125;&#125; = 60 \times 4 = 240 \) kN&middot;m.
        <div class="eq">\[ M = \frac&#123;100 \times 8.70^2&#125;&#123;15&#125; - 240 = 504.6 - 240 = 264.6\ \text&#123;kN·m&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Shear.</strong> Push the train up to A with the 60 kN axle on the support: \( V_A = 100\dfrac&#123;15-1.6&#125;&#123;15&#125; = 89.3 \) kN. The alternative &mdash; running the 60 kN off the end and bringing the 40 kN to A &mdash; leaves only 40 kN on the span. Worse.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \(M_&#123;\text&#123;abs&#125;&#125; = 299.3\) kN&middot;m under the 60 kN axle, positioned 6.70 m from A (0.80 m left of midspan). \(V_&#123;\text&#123;abs&#125;&#125; = 89.3\) kN at the support. The heavier axle nearest the resultant governs, as expected.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 2</span><h4>Three-Axle Train on a 20 m Span &mdash; Where the Sign of e Decides It</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> Axle loads of 40 kN, 60 kN and 20 kN with spacings of 4 m and 3 m cross a 20 m simply supported beam. Find the absolute maximum moment.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Resultant.</strong> \( R = 120 \) kN. Measuring from the 40 kN axle (\(d = 0, 4, 7\) m):
        <div class="eq">\[ \bar&#123;x&#125; = \frac&#123;40(0)+60(4)+20(7)&#125;&#123;120&#125; = \frac&#123;380&#125;&#123;120&#125; = 3.167\ \text&#123;m&#125; \]</div>
        The resultant falls between axles 1 and 2, nearest to axle 2 (0.833 m away).</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Candidate 1 &mdash; the 40 kN axle.</strong> \( e = 3.167 \), \( x = \dfrac&#123;20-3.167&#125;&#123;2&#125; = 8.417 \) m, \( M_&#123;\text&#123;left&#125;&#125; = 0 \):
        <div class="eq">\[ M_1 = \frac&#123;120 \times 8.417^2&#125;&#123;20&#125; = 425.0\ \text&#123;kN·m&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Candidate 2 &mdash; the 60 kN axle.</strong> R is to its <em>left</em>, so \( e = 3.167 - 4 = -0.833 \) m and \( x = \dfrac&#123;20+0.833&#125;&#123;2&#125; = 10.417 \) m &mdash; just past midspan. \( M_&#123;\text&#123;left&#125;&#125; = 40 \times 4 = 160 \) kN&middot;m:
        <div class="eq">\[ M_2 = \frac&#123;120 \times 10.417^2&#125;&#123;20&#125; - 160 = 651.0 - 160 = 491.0\ \text&#123;kN·m&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Candidate 3 &mdash; the 20 kN axle.</strong> \( e = 3.167-7 = -3.833 \) m, \( x = 11.917 \) m, \( M_&#123;\text&#123;left&#125;&#125; = 40(7)+60(3) = 460 \) kN&middot;m:
        <div class="eq">\[ M_3 = \frac&#123;120 \times 11.917^2&#125;&#123;20&#125; - 460 = 852.1 - 460 = 392.1\ \text&#123;kN·m&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Legality.</strong> For candidate 2 the axles sit at 6.417, 10.417 and 13.417 m &mdash; all inside the span. Valid.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \(M_&#123;\text&#123;abs&#125;&#125; = 491.0\) kN&middot;m, under the 60 kN axle at x = 10.42 m, with the leading axle 6.42 m from A. Note that dropping the minus sign on e would have put the 60 kN axle at 9.58 m and given 391.0 kN&middot;m &mdash; the answer would then have looked <em>smaller</em> than candidate 1, and you would have reported the wrong governing axle as well as the wrong number.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 3</span><h4>How Good Is "Just Check Midspan"?</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> For the Example 2 train, compare the true absolute maximum against the largest moment obtainable at midspan.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div>Absolute maximum, from Example 2: <strong>491.0 kN&middot;m</strong> at x = 10.42 m.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div>Sweep the train and record the best moment at x = 10.00 m exactly: <strong>490.0 kN&middot;m</strong>.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div>Difference: \( \dfrac&#123;491.0-490.0&#125;&#123;491.0&#125; = 0.2\% \).</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> The midspan check is low by 0.2% &mdash; far inside any load factor, which is why practising engineers often use it and why the envelope's peak is so flat. Use it as a sanity check on your exact answer: if your bisection-rule result differs from the midspan value by more than a percent or two, you have made an arithmetic slip.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 9 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-quiz">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">09</span><h2>Quick Reference &amp; Quick Check</h2></div>

  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>Envelope</span><span>max moment at each section over all load positions</span></div>
    <div><span>Envelope, single load P</span><span>Px(L&minus;x)/L &mdash; a parabola</span></div>
    <div><span>Peak of envelope</span><span>the absolute maximum moment</span></div>
    <div><span>Peak location</span><span>slightly off midspan, toward the resultant</span></div>
    <div><span>Max moment sits</span><span>always under an axle (BMD is straight between axles)</span></div>
    <div><span>Resultant</span><span>R = &Sigma;P&#7522;, x&#772; = &Sigma;P&#7522;d&#7522;/R</span></div>
    <div><span>Eccentricity</span><span>e = x&#772; &minus; d&#8340; — <b>signed</b>, + if R is right of P&#8340;</span></div>
    <div><span>Bisection rule</span><span>midspan bisects the gap between P&#8340; and R</span></div>
    <div><span>Position of P&#8340;</span><span>x = (L &minus; e)/2 from A</span></div>
    <div><span>Candidate moment</span><span>M&#8340; = Rx&sup2;/L &minus; M<sub>left</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>M<sub>left</sub></span><span>moment about P&#8340; of the axles ahead of it</span></div>
    <div><span>Best first guess</span><span>the axle nearest the resultant</span></div>
    <div><span>Legality check</span><span>whole train must stay on the span at x</span></div>
    <div><span>Absolute max shear</span><span>largest reaction; train shoved against a support</span></div>
    <div><span>Midspan shortcut</span><span>within ~0.2% for typical trains, but not exact</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" id="quiz">
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>1. Why can the absolute maximum moment only occur directly under an axle?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Because the reaction is largest there</button>
      <button data-v="1">Because the BMD is a straight line between axles, so its peak must be at a kink</button>
      <button data-v="2">Because the resultant always coincides with an axle</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>2. The bisection rule positions the train so that:</p>
      <button data-v="0">The resultant sits exactly at midspan</button>
      <button data-v="1">The heaviest axle sits exactly at midspan</button>
      <button data-v="2">Midspan lies halfway between the candidate axle and the resultant</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="0">
      <p>3. The resultant of a train lies 0.83 m to the <em>left</em> of your candidate axle on a 20 m span. Where does that axle go?</p>
      <button data-v="0">To x = 10.42 m — past midspan, since e is negative</button>
      <button data-v="1">To x = 9.58 m — before midspan</button>
      <button data-v="2">To x = 10.00 m — exactly at midspan</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>4. Your bisection-rule position puts the rear axle 0.5 m beyond support B. What do you do?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Use it anyway — the overhanging load helps by reducing the moment</button>
      <button data-v="1">Discard that candidate as invalid and check the others, or fall back on a direct search</button>
      <button data-v="2">Shift the train back until it fits and use the moment there</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>5. Why is the envelope useful even though no single load position ever produces it?</p>
      <button data-v="0">It shows the deflected shape of the beam under the worst load</button>
      <button data-v="1">It is the BMD for the train parked at midspan</button>
      <button data-v="2">It gives the design demand at every section at once — the moment each section must resist</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="quiz-score" class="quiz-score"></div>
</section>

<footer class="chapter-nav">
  <a href="/civil/cee330/arches-cables">&larr; Arches &amp; Cables</a>
  <a href="/civil">Back to Civil Engineering &rarr;</a>
</footer>

<script is:inline>
(function(){
  const g=id=>document.getElementById(id), v=id=>+g(id).value;

  function train(){
    const L=Math.max(4,v('mv-L'));
    const s1=Math.max(0.1,v('mv-s1')), s2=Math.max(0.1,v('mv-s2'));
    const raw=[[v('mv-p1'),0],[v('mv-p2'),s1],[v('mv-p3'),s1+s2]];
    const ax=raw.filter(a=>a[0]>0).map(a=>({P:a[0],d:a[1]}));
    return {L,ax,len:ax.length?ax[ax.length-1].d-ax[0].d:0,off:ax.length?ax[0].d:0};
  }
  // reactions + moment for the train with axle-1 datum at x0
  function state(L,ax,x0){
    const on=ax.map(a=>({P:a.P,x:x0+a.d})).filter(a=>a.x>=-1e-9&&a.x<=L+1e-9);
    const R=on.reduce((s,a)=>s+a.P,0);
    const VA=R?on.reduce((s,a)=>s+a.P*(L-a.x),0)/L:0;
    const M=t=>VA*t-on.reduce((s,a)=>s+(a.x<t?a.P*(t-a.x):0),0);
    return {on,R,VA,VB:R-VA,M};
  }
  function candidates(L,ax){
    const R=ax.reduce((s,a)=>s+a.P,0);
    if(!R) return {R:0,xbar:0,rows:[],best:null};
    const xbar=ax.reduce((s,a)=>s+a.P*a.d,0)/R;
    const span=ax[ax.length-1].d-ax[0].d;
    const rows=ax.map((a,k)=>{
      const e=xbar-a.d, x=(L-e)/2;
      const Ml=ax.reduce((s,b)=>s+(b.d<a.d?b.P*(a.d-b.d):0),0);
      const M=R*x*x/L-Ml;
      const x0=x-a.d, first=x0+ax[0].d, last=first+span;
      return {k:k+1,P:a.P,e,Ml,x,M,x0,ok:first>=-1e-9&&last<=L+1e-9};
    });
    const valid=rows.filter(r=>r.ok&&r.M>0);
    const best=valid.length?valid.reduce((a,b)=>b.M>a.M?b:a):null;
    return {R,xbar,rows,best};
  }
  // envelope + absolute max shear, cached on the train definition
  let cache={key:'',env:[],peak:0,peakX:0,Vmax:0};
  function envelope(L,ax){
    const key=L+'|'+ax.map(a=>a.P+'@'+a.d).join(',');
    if(cache.key===key) return cache;
    const NS=300, NP=1200, lo=-(ax.length?ax[ax.length-1].d:0), hi=L;
    const env=new Array(NS+1).fill(0);
    for(let j=0;j<=NP;j++){
      const st=state(L,ax,lo+(hi-lo)*j/NP);
      if(!st.R) continue;
      for(let i=0;i<=NS;i++){const m=st.M(L*i/NS); if(m>env[i])env[i]=m;}
    }
    let peak=0,peakX=0;
    env.forEach((m,i)=>{if(m>peak){peak=m;peakX=L*i/NS;}});
    // Max reaction is exact, not sampled: it can only occur with some axle
    // sitting right on a support, so test those configurations directly.
    let Vmax=0;
    ax.forEach(a=>{
      [[-a.d,'A'],[L-a.d,'B']].forEach(([x0,end])=>{
        const st=state(L,ax,x0);
        const V=end==='A'?st.VA:st.VB;
        if(V>Vmax)Vmax=V;
      });
    });
    cache={key,env,peak,peakX,Vmax,NS};
    return cache;
  }

  function draw(){
    const {L,ax}=train();
    const cd=candidates(L,ax), ev=envelope(L,ax);
    const span=ax.length?ax[ax.length-1].d-ax[0].d:0;
    const lo=-span, hi=L;
    const x0=lo+(hi-lo)*v('mv-x')/1000;
    g('mv-xv').textContent=(x0+(ax.length?ax[0].d:0)).toFixed(2);
    const st=state(L,ax,x0);

    // current maximum, which always sits under an axle
    let curM=0,curX=0;
    st.on.forEach(a=>{const m=st.M(a.x); if(m>curM){curM=m;curX=a.x;}});

    g('mv-res').innerHTML=
      `<div class="res"><span>Load on span R</span><b>${st.R.toFixed(0)} kN of ${ax.reduce((s,a)=>s+a.P,0).toFixed(0)} kN</b></div>`+
      `<div class="res"><span>Reactions V<sub>A</sub> / V<sub>B</sub></span><b>${st.VA.toFixed(1)} / ${st.VB.toFixed(1)} kN</b></div>`+
      `<div class="res"><span>Max moment, this position</span><b>${curM.toFixed(1)} kN&middot;m at x = ${curX.toFixed(2)} m</b></div>`+
      (cd.best
        ? `<div class="verdict ${Math.abs(curM-cd.best.M)<0.05*Math.max(1,cd.best.M)?'v-pass':'v-fail'}"><span>Absolute maximum (axle ${cd.best.k})</span><b>${cd.best.M.toFixed(1)} kN&middot;m at x = ${cd.best.x.toFixed(2)} m</b></div>`
        : `<div class="verdict v-fail"><span>Absolute maximum</span><b>no valid candidate — see &sect;7</b></div>`)+
      `<div class="res"><span>Envelope peak (direct search)</span><b>${ev.peak.toFixed(1)} kN&middot;m at x = ${ev.peakX.toFixed(2)} m</b></div>`+
      `<div class="res"><span>Absolute max shear</span><b>${ev.Vmax.toFixed(1)} kN</b></div>`;

    g('mv-tab').innerHTML=cd.rows.map(r=>
      `<div class="res"><span>Axle ${r.k} &nbsp;(${r.P} kN) &nbsp; e = ${r.e>=0?'+':''}${r.e.toFixed(3)} m, M<sub>left</sub> = ${r.Ml.toFixed(0)}</span>`+
      `<b style="color:${!r.ok||r.M<=0?'var(--mut)':(cd.best&&r.k===cd.best.k?'var(--grn)':'var(--txt2)')}">`+
      `${!r.ok?'invalid — train off span':(r.M<=0?'not governing':'x = '+r.x.toFixed(2)+' m &rarr; '+r.M.toFixed(1)+' kN&middot;m'+(cd.best&&r.k===cd.best.k?'  &#9733;':''))}</b></div>`
    ).join('')||'<div class="res"><span>No axles defined</span><b>&mdash;</b></div>';

    const W=340,padL=30,padR=16, X=t=>padL+(t/L)*(W-padL-padR);
    const topH=96, momH=92, Ht=topH+momH;
    const beamY=68;
    let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${Ht}" style="width:100%;height:auto">`;
    s+=`<defs><marker id="mvh" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 Z" fill="var(--rust)"/></marker>`+
       `<marker id="mvt" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 Z" fill="var(--teal)"/></marker></defs>`;
    // beam + supports
    s+=`<line x1="${X(0)}" y1="${beamY}" x2="${X(L)}" y2="${beamY}" stroke="var(--txt2)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;
    [0,L].forEach(t=>s+=`<polygon points="${X(t)},${beamY} ${X(t)-6},${beamY+9} ${X(t)+6},${beamY+9}" fill="var(--bdr2)" stroke="var(--txt2)"/>`);
    s+=`<text x="${X(0)}" y="${beamY+20}" font-size="7.5" fill="var(--teal)" text-anchor="middle">${st.VA.toFixed(0)}</text>`;
    s+=`<text x="${X(L)}" y="${beamY+20}" font-size="7.5" fill="var(--teal)" text-anchor="middle">${st.VB.toFixed(0)}</text>`;
    s+=`<line x1="${X(L/2)}" y1="${beamY-46}" x2="${X(L/2)}" y2="${beamY+18}" stroke="var(--bdr2)" stroke-width=".8" stroke-dasharray="2,3"/>`;
    s+=`<text x="${X(L/2)}" y="${beamY+26}" font-size="7" fill="var(--mut)" text-anchor="middle">midspan</text>`;
    // axles
    ax.forEach(a=>{
      const xa=x0+a.d, onSpan=xa>=0&&xa<=L;
      if(xa<-0.8||xa>L+0.8) return;          // well off the beam — don't crowd the edge
      const px=X(xa);
      s+=`<line x1="${px}" y1="${beamY-34}" x2="${px}" y2="${beamY-3}" stroke="${onSpan?'var(--rust)':'var(--mut)'}" stroke-width="2" opacity="${onSpan?1:.35}" marker-end="url(#mvh)"/>`;
      s+=`<text x="${px}" y="${beamY-37}" font-size="7.5" fill="${onSpan?'var(--rust)':'var(--mut)'}" text-anchor="middle" opacity="${onSpan?1:.5}">${a.P}</text>`;
    });
    // resultant
    if(cd.R){const xr=x0+cd.xbar;
      if(xr>=-1&&xr<=L+1){s+=`<line x1="${X(xr)}" y1="${beamY-58}" x2="${X(xr)}" y2="${beamY-40}" stroke="var(--vio)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#mvh)" opacity=".9"/>`;
        s+=`<text x="${X(xr)}" y="${beamY-61}" font-size="7.5" fill="var(--vio)" text-anchor="middle">R = ${cd.R}</text>`;}}

    // moment panel: envelope ghosted, current BMD solid
    const mtop=topH+6, mmid=mtop+20, amp=momH-38;
    const sc=Math.max(ev.peak,1e-6), Ym=m=>mmid+amp-(m/sc)*amp;
    s+=`<line x1="${X(0)}" y1="${Ym(0)}" x2="${X(L)}" y2="${Ym(0)}" stroke="var(--bdr2)" stroke-width="1"/>`;
    let ep=`M ${X(0)} ${Ym(0)}`;
    ev.env.forEach((m,i)=>{ep+=` L ${X(L*i/ev.NS).toFixed(1)} ${Ym(m).toFixed(1)}`;});
    ep+=` L ${X(L)} ${Ym(0)} Z`;
    s+=`<path d="${ep}" fill="var(--teal)" opacity=".13" stroke="var(--teal)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>`;
    let bp=`M ${X(0)} ${Ym(0)}`;
    const pts=[0,...st.on.map(a=>a.x),L].sort((a,b)=>a-b);
    pts.forEach(t=>{bp+=` L ${X(t).toFixed(1)} ${Ym(st.M(t)).toFixed(1)}`;});
    s+=`<path d="${bp}" fill="none" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="2.2"/>`;
    if(curM>0){s+=`<circle cx="${X(curX)}" cy="${Ym(curM)}" r="3" fill="var(--rust)"/>`;
      s+=`<text x="${X(curX)}" y="${Ym(curM)-6}" font-size="7.5" fill="var(--rust)" text-anchor="middle">${curM.toFixed(0)}</text>`;}
    s+=`<circle cx="${X(ev.peakX)}" cy="${Ym(ev.peak)}" r="2.6" fill="none" stroke="var(--grn)" stroke-width="1.6"/>`;
    s+=`<text x="${padL}" y="${mtop+4}" font-size="7.5" fill="var(--txt2)">BMD now (solid) vs envelope (shaded)</text>`;
    s+=`<text x="${W-padR}" y="${mtop+4}" font-size="7.5" fill="var(--grn)" text-anchor="end">&#9675; peak ${ev.peak.toFixed(0)}</text>`;
    s+=`</svg>`;
    g('mv-fig').innerHTML=s;
  }

  ['mv-L','mv-p1','mv-p2','mv-p3','mv-s1','mv-s2','mv-x'].forEach(id=>g(id).addEventListener('input',draw));
  g('mv-snap').addEventListener('click',()=>{
    const {L,ax}=train(); const cd=candidates(L,ax);
    const span=ax.length?ax[ax.length-1].d-ax[0].d:0, lo=-span, hi=L;
    const ev=envelope(L,ax);
    // use the rule's position when a candidate is valid, else the direct search
    let x0=cd.best?cd.best.x0:null;
    if(x0===null){
      let bm=-1;
      for(let j=0;j<=600;j++){const t=lo+(hi-lo)*j/600, st=state(L,ax,t);
        let m=0; st.on.forEach(a=>{m=Math.max(m,st.M(a.x));});
        if(m>bm){bm=m;x0=t;}}
    }
    g('mv-x').value=Math.round((x0-lo)/(hi-lo)*1000);
    draw();
  });
  draw();

  document.querySelectorAll('.quiz .q').forEach(q=>{
    const btns=q.querySelectorAll('button');
    btns.forEach(btn=>btn.addEventListener('click',()=>{
      if(q.classList.contains('answered'))return;
      q.classList.add('answered');
      if(btn.dataset.v===q.dataset.a)q.classList.add('gotit');
      btns.forEach(b=>{ if(b.dataset.v===q.dataset.a)b.classList.add('correct'); else if(b===btn)b.classList.add('wrong'); });
      const A=document.querySelectorAll('.quiz .q.answered').length;
      const R=document.querySelectorAll('.quiz .q.gotit').length;
      g('quiz-score').textContent=R+' / '+A+' answered correctly so far';
    }));
  });
})();
</script>

</Base>
